  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "In the restaurant, Nastya stops Kostya because she wants to have a very serious conversation, and we will listen to their dialogue in Russian first; do not worry about catching every single word.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Костя, нам надо поговорить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Это может подождать?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нет, это важно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Настя, я так по тебе соскучился.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Пожалуйста, послушай меня.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Я тебя слушаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Наши отношения развиваются быстро.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Я думаю, это отлично.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Но нам надо обсудить наше будущее.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "У нас есть много времени.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нет, время пришло сейчас.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Хорошо, я готов поговорить.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Let us break down this interaction.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Костя, нам надо поговорить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Kostya, we have to talk.' Russian expresses this impersonally, literally saying 'to me' or 'to you' with 'to talk'. Then Kostya replies.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Это может подождать?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Can it wait?' Russian forms this yes-or-no question without a question word, relying on rising pitch on 'to wait'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нет, это важно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'No, it is important.' Notice how 'no' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'important'. Then Kostya responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Настя, я так по тебе соскучился.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Nastya, I have missed you so much.' Notice the reflexive ending on the verb for 'missed', which directs the action back onto the subject.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Пожалуйста, послушай меня.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Please, listen to me.' Nastya combines 'please' with 'me' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Я тебя слушаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am listening to you.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'I' with 'I'm listening'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Наши отношения развиваются быстро.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Our relationship is moving fast.' Notice the reflexive ending on the verb for 'develop', which directs the action back onto the subject. Then Kostya responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Я думаю, это отлично.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I think this is great.' Kostya links 'I' directly with 'I think' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Но нам надо обсудить наше будущее.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'But we need to discuss our future.' Nastya links 'but' directly with 'us' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "У нас есть много времени.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We have a lot of time.' Kostya combines 'at' with 'time' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нет, время пришло сейчас.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'No, the time is now.' Nastya combines 'no' with 'now' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Kostya responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Хорошо, я готов поговорить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Okay, I am ready to talk.' Kostya links 'good' directly with 'I' to express the thought clearly. Now let us listen to the conversation again without interruption.",
      "gap": 0.9
